    "q": "1. What is signal integrity and how do you maintain it in high-speed digital designs?",
    "whatISay": "Signal integrity is the ability of an electrical signal to travel from a transmitter to a receiver cleanly enough that the receiver samples the right logic level at the right clock edge—without reflections, crosstalk, overshoot, or ground bounce corrupting the waveform. In real post-silicon systems, signal integrity is inseparable from power integrity, because if the power delivery network drops during a heavy current transient, transistor switching slows down and noise margins shrink. At Google on CPU2 workload profiling, I reviewed schematics to locate VDD_CPU2, VDD_CPU2_M, current-sense points, and CLKOUT1, and used a 4-channel Keysight oscilloscope across MaxPower, Unzip, SVE_MCOPY, and FFT_NEON to capture how 110 mV of supply droop correlated with current spikes and clock behavior.",
    "deepTech": "High-speed edges contain high-frequency harmonics (f_knee ≈ 0.5 / t_rise), so PCB traces behave as transmission lines rather than simple wires. Whenever a trace encounters an impedance discontinuity—such as a via stub, socket contactor, package ball, or missing return-plane reference—part of the wave reflects back (Γ = (Z_L − Z0) / (Z_L + Z0)), causing ringing and inter-symbol interference (ISI) that closes the data eye. Simultaneously, rapid current steps (dI/dt) across package and board inductance L create inductive voltage droop (V_L = L · dI/dt) on the supply rail. Maintaining SI/PI requires: (1) controlled trace impedance (typically 50 Ω single-ended, 85–100 Ω differential) with proper ODT/series termination; (2) unbroken ground reference planes so return current follows directly beneath the signal trace; (3) tight length matching and spacing (≥ 3W rule) to limit skew and crosstalk; and (4) low-inductance decoupling capacitors placed right at the SoC package.",
