    "q": "3. What is setup and hold time? What happens if these requirements are not met?",
    "whatISay": "Setup time is how long data must be stable before the capturing flip-flop's clock edge, and hold time is how long data must remain stable after that clock edge. If either is violated, the flip-flop can latch the wrong bit or enter metastability—hovering at an indeterminate voltage before settling unpredictably—which causes silent data corruption or random system hangs. At 4.112 GHz in my Google CPU2 DVFS0 testing, one clock period is only 243 picoseconds, so a 110 mV supply droop during MaxPower can slow combinational logic enough to eat right into setup margin.",
    "deepTech": "Mathematically, setup requires: T_clk ≥ T_clk_to_Q + T_comb_max + t_su − T_skew. Notice that clock period T_clk is on the left side—so lowering clock frequency (increasing T_clk) or raising VDD (reducing T_comb_max) gives you more setup margin. Hold requires: T_clk_to_Q + T_comb_min ≥ t_h + T_skew. Notice T_clk does not appear in the hold equation at all! Hold is checked on the same clock edge, which means lowering the frequency will never fix a hold violation; hold violations are fixed by adding buffer delay to fast paths, reducing clock skew, or adjusting PVT/corner timing. In my SFSU ENGR 852 14nm ASIC project, we signed off PrimeTime STA across 3 PVT corners (+0.71 ns / +0.64 ns / +0.22 ns setup slack and ≥ 0.00 ns hold slack) specifically to guarantee both bounds.",
