    "q": "7. Explain the role of a PHY layer in PCIe communication.",
    "whatISay": "The PCIe Physical Layer—or PHY—is the bottom layer of the PCIe stack that translates parallel Transaction and Data Link Layer packets into high-speed serial differential signals over copper traces, connectors, or risers, and recovers them cleanly at the receiver. It handles serialization and deserialization (SerDes), encoding (8b/10b for Gen1/2 or 128b/130b for Gen3/4/5), scrambling to reduce EMI, clock and data recovery (CDR), lane-to-lane deskew, and the LTSSM state machine that trains the link up to its maximum speed and width.",
    "deepTech": "PCIe splits the PHY into two sub-blocks: (1) The Logical Sub-block, which stripes bytes across x1/x4/x8/x16 lanes, scrambles data with an LFSR to guarantee transitions and spread spectral energy, applies 8b/10b (20% overhead) or 128b/130b (~1.5% overhead) encoding, and runs the LTSSM (Link Training and Status State Machine: Detect → Polling → Configuration → L0, plus Recovery for speed changes/equalization); and (2) The Electrical Sub-block, which includes differential TX drivers with programmable pre-shoot and de-emphasis to counteract channel insertion loss, AC coupling capacitors, and RX front-ends with CTLE (Continuous Time Linear Equalization), DFE (Decision Feedback Equalization), and a CDR PLL that extracts the embedded clock from the data stream (since PCIe has no forwarded clock lane).",
