    "q": "8. How is a DDR4/DDR5 interface different from LPDDR?",
    "whatISay": "DDR4 and DDR5 are designed for high-capacity, modular desktop and server systems using DIMM slots and higher signaling voltages, while LPDDR4/4X and LPDDR5/5X are designed for power efficiency and dense soldered-down or PoP integration in mobile and AI SoCs. LPDDR achieves much lower power by shrinking I/O voltage swings, splitting the bus into narrower independent sub-channels, adding aggressive low-power states, and using a dedicated write clock (WCK) in LPDDR5.",
    "deepTech": "Key architectural differences: I/O Voltage & Power: DDR4 uses 1.2 V VDD/VDDQ (POD12 termination); DDR5 drops to 1.1 V with an on-DIMM PMIC. LPDDR4X drops I/O VDDQ to 0.6 V, and LPDDR5 drops VDDQ further to 0.5 V (since dynamic I/O power scales as C · V² · f, halving V cuts switching power by 4×). Channel Organization: DDR4 has one 64-bit (+8-bit ECC) channel per DIMM; DDR5 splits each DIMM into two independent 32-bit (+8-bit ECC) sub-channels; LPDDR4/5 uses narrow 16-bit independent channels (often 4–8 channels per SoC). Clocking & Features: LPDDR5 introduces WCK (2× or 4× CK), Link-ECC, Deep Sleep, PASR, and dynamic DVFS frequency set-points (FSP) requiring tight PHY ZQCal across PF states.",
