    "q": "12. A USB 3.0 port works in USB 2.0 mode but not in 3.0 — what's your debug path?",
    "whatISay": "Because USB 3.0 uses completely separate SuperSpeed differential pairs (SSTX± and SSRX±) while keeping USB 2.0 on the legacy D+ and D− pins, the fact that USB 2.0 works immediately tells me VBUS power, ground, and basic host controller enumeration are healthy. My debug immediately narrows to the SuperSpeed physical path, AC coupling capacitors, ESD diodes, retimer/redriver, or xHCI/PHY SuperSpeed link training.",
    "deepTech": "USB 3.0/3.1 (5 Gbps / 10 Gbps) runs over SSTX± and SSRX± and performs RxDetect (the TX looks for 50 Ω far-end receiver termination through 0.1 µF AC coupling caps) followed by Polling.LFPS (Low-Frequency Periodic Signaling) and Gen1/Gen2 equalization training. If RxDetect fails—due to a missing/tombstoned AC coupling capacitor on SSTX, a bent SuperSpeed pin in the receptacle, or a P/N polarity/TX-RX swap on the PCB—the port immediately falls back to USB 2.0 on D+/D−. Excessive parasitic capacitance from wrong ESD diodes or missing SuperSpeed redriver enable/strap pins can also kill LFPS or close the 5 Gbps eye.",
    "babySteps": [
      "Known-good A/B test: swap the USB 3.0 cable and peripheral first to confirm the issue stays on the DUT port.",
      "Linux check: run lsusb -t (verify 480M vs 5000M/10000M) and dmesg | grep -iE 'usb|xhci' for cannot enable port or SuperSpeed link-training errors.",
      "Microscope & continuity check: inspect the connector's inner SuperSpeed pins (pins 5–9 on Type-A, TX/RX on Type-C) and verify the 0.1 µF AC coupling caps on SSTX± are soldered and not tombstoned.",
      "Schematic & scope check: verify SSRX/SSTX routing isn't swapped at the connector/retimer, check retimer/redriver power & I2C config, and probe SSTX± for LFPS bursts during plug-in."
    ],
    "formulas": [
      "USB3_5G_Overhead: 8b/10b -> 4.0 Gbps effective vs USB2 480 Mbps"
    ],
    "visualKind": "usb3-lfps"
