    "whatISay": "I validate an Ethernet PHY in three layers from bottom up: first, electrical bring-up (rails, 25/50/125 MHz reference clock, reset release, and hardware strap pins); second, management & MAC-to-PHY link (reading PHY ID and link registers over MDIO/MDC and checking RGMII/SGMII/USXGMII timing); and third, live traffic & physical compliance (running internal/external loopback and iperf3 while monitoring ethtool -S for zero CRC/FCS errors).",
    "deepTech": "Many board-level Ethernet failures happen in two classic places: (1) Strap pins sampled at reset de-assertion (determining PHY address, auto-negotiation, and RGMII internal clock delay—wrong pull-up/down values leave the PHY silent on MDIO or mis-timed); and (2) RGMII timing / MDI magnetics, where missing 2 ns TX/RX clock delay on RGMII causes packet loss at 1000 Mbps while 100 Mbps still works, or a mis-wired transformer center-tap bias breaks voltage-mode/current-mode MDI signaling. At Cisco on Silicon One, checking L1/SerDes and FCS counters immediately separated MDIO/strap config errors from physical channel/SI loss.",
