    "q": "14. Walk me through how you'd bring up and test an I2C sensor.",
    "whatISay": "To bring up an I2C sensor, I start on the schematic—verifying the sensor's VCC rail, 7-bit address strap pins, and pull-up resistors on SDA and SCL. Next, I check the idle bus voltage and waveform rise times with an oscilloscope. Then in Linux or firmware, I scan the bus with i2cdetect, read the fixed WHO_AM_I / Device ID register to prove clean ACK/NACK communication, and finally verify the actual telemetry reading against a known physical stimulus like a calibrated RTD or thermal head.",
    "deepTech": "I2C uses open-drain drivers, meaning devices can only pull SDA/SCL low—external pull-up resistors (R_p, typically 2.2 kΩ–4.7 kΩ) are what pull the lines back high. If R_p is missing or too large for the bus capacitance (τ = R_p × C_bus), rising edges become rounded and fail setup/hold at 400 kHz Fast Mode. Other classic bring-up traps include 1.8 V vs 3.3 V level-shifter directionality, 7-bit vs 8-bit address confusion (0x48 vs 0x90), or a mid-transaction reset leaving a slave holding SDA stuck low (requiring a 9-clock recovery toggle on SCL).",
