    "q": "17. What's the typical DDR training sequence during boot?",
    "whatISay": "At cold power-on, external DRAM cannot hold data until the Memory Reference Code (MRC) or DRAM initialization firmware calibrates the PHY interface to the physical board traces. The sequence runs in four main stages: first, DRAM reset and ZQ impedance calibration (ZQCL) against an external precision resistor; second, Command/Address (CA) training relative to the clock; third, Write Leveling and Read/Write DQS-to-DQ 2D eye centering; and fourth, locking the calibrated parameters and running a quick BIST before unlocking the NoC and booting the OS.",
    "deepTech": "In modern SoCs, early boot runs out of tiny internal SRAM (BL2), which fetches a signed DRAM init binary (such as dram_init_7_usb_signed.bin / did7 matched to the DRAM vendor ID). The training FSM executes: (1) ZQCal (PCODE PMOS pull-up / NCODE NMOS pull-down) against a 240 Ω reference resistor so driver impedance matches PCB trace Z0 (~40–50 Ω); (2) CA Training so Read/Write/Precharge commands are sampled cleanly; (3) Write Leveling & DQS-DQ Shmoo, sweeping picosecond phase delays and millivolt Vref (SMV1D/SMV2D) with checkerboard/PRBS patterns to map the 2D data eye; and (4) Eye Locking (Status 7 pass vs Status ff fail), which commits the DRAM address range to the NoC decoders and lets the CPU enable its MMU.",
