    "whatISay": "ATE—like Advantest 93K with SmarTest 8 that I ran at Intel and Cisco—uses deterministic high-speed pin electronics to screen structural faults such as stuck-at scan, transition delay, MBIST, IDDQ, and DC/AC parametrics in just seconds per part. System Level Testing puts that packaged SoC into a real motherboard or platform booting actual firmware and Linux, running concurrent CPU, GPU, PCIe, and DRAM workloads over minutes or hours to catch functional, thermal, and multi-domain timing escapes that ATE patterns cannot reproduce.",
    "deepTech": "ATE tests blocks largely in isolation under controlled tester power supplies and rigid vector timing. Real systems introduce: (1) simultaneous switching noise across multiple domains (CPU + GPU + NoC + DRAM) causing first-droop IR collapse; (2) dynamic DVFS/PF-state transitions and real PMIC settling loops; (3) full protocol state machines (PCIe Gen4/5 LTSSM equalization, DDR MRC 2D training); and (4) self-heating thermal gradients. Because SLT takes minutes per unit while ATE takes seconds, SLT's mission is both final quality gating and feeding escape signatures back to DFT/ATE to tighten structural screens.",
