    "sectionTitle": "Coding QA (25 Python/Bench Guides)",
    "q": "Question 17: Difference between assert statements and raise exceptions for error handling in tests?",
    "whatISay": "assert is for pytest test checks and internal invariants—and Python strips all assert statements completely when run with -O! For runtime hardware safety limits (over-temperature, invalid voltage, VISA timeout), always use explicit if ...: raise ValueError(...) so safety guards can never be optimized away.",
    "deepTech": "A thermal runaway clamp written as `assert temp < 105` completely vanishes if an operator launches `python3 -O run_test.py`! Explicit `raise` guarantees fail-safe shutdown regardless of interpreter flags.",
    "babySteps": [
      "Grep production automation drivers for bare assert on safety/voltage checks and replace with explicit raise."
    ],
    "formulas": [
      "python -O strips assert! Never guard HW safety with assert"
    ],
