    "whatISay": "First I confirm the reference unit passes on its known-good setup. Next, once the suspect fixture is electrically safe, I test the reference unit on the suspect fixture—if it fails there too, the fixture/board path is suspect. Where DRAM and board can be separated, I run an A-B-A swap: suspect DRAM on known-good board, and known-good DRAM on suspect board—keeping each SKU's compatible firmware fixed.",
    "deepTech": "Controlled A-B-A substitution isolates whether a defect follows the DUT or stays with the station. If suspect DRAM fails on both setups while reference DRAM passes on both, the fault follows the DRAM assembly. If both fail only on the original fixture, the shared power/routing/socket path is guilty. Note that if SoC and DRAM are co-packaged or move together as one assembly, swapping that assembly isolates the combined package—not the DRAM die independently.",
