    "id": "fn-q7",
    "section": "fundamental-math",
    "sectionTitle": "Fundamental EE & DRAM Math (Q1–Q8)",
    "q": "Fund Q7: Explain the transmission-line reflection coefficient Γ and what happens at an open, short, or matched load.",
    "whatISay": "The reflection coefficient Γ equals (Z_L − Z0) divided by (Z_L + Z0), giving the fraction of a signal wave reflected back from a load impedance Z_L on a trace of characteristic impedance Z0. When Z_L matches Z0, Γ = 0 (no reflection). At an open circuit, Γ = +1 (full positive reflection, doubling voltage). At a short, Γ = −1 (full inverted reflection).",
    "deepTech": "At high frequencies (PCIe, DDR/LPDDR DQ/CK), PCB traces behave as transmission lines rather than lumped wires. Any impedance discontinuity—wrong ODT (On-Die Termination), missing termination resistor, via stub, or bad socket pin—creates non-zero Γ. Reflected energy bounces back and forth, causing overshoot, undershoot, non-monotonic edges (ledges), and inter-symbol interference (ISI) that closes the data eye.",
    "babySteps": [
      "Look for stepped/ledge edges or severe overshoot/undershoot on the scope at the receiver.",
      "Verify ODT/ZQ calibration settings (PCODE/NCODE) and trace termination resistors against Z0 (e.g., 50 Ω single-ended, 85–100 Ω differential)."
    ],
    "formulas": [
      "Gamma = (Z_L - Z_0) / (Z_L + Z_0)"
    ],
    "visualKind": "si-pi-droop"
