    "question": "When measuring microvolts across a `1.0 mΩ` precision current shunt (carrying `1 A` = `1.000 mV`) or testing socket pin continuity down to `5 mΩ`, you notice turning the current OFF still leaves a persistent `+35 µV` reading shifting as your hand touches the probes! Where did `35 µV` come from with zero current, and how do high-precision meters cancel it?",
