    "unit": "concept",
    "unitConv": "Explain with exact SI/EE units, physical mechanism, and bench verification step.",
    "steps": [
      "Step 1 (Eliminate `O(N)` Giant RAM Explosion): Replace `fp.read().splitlines()` with lazy file iteration (`with open(path, \"r\", buffering=1024*1024) as f: for line in f:`) — uses constant ~1 MB RAM even on a 50 GB log!",
      "Step 2 (Eliminate `O(N^2)` Sliding Window Scanning): Instead of checking every earlier timestamp on each match (`nested for`), maintain `from collections import deque; win = deque()`.",
      "Step 3 (Maintain Amortized `O(1)` Per Line!): On each new error at timestamp `ts`, `win.append(ts)`, then `while win and (ts - win[0] > 10.0): win.popleft()`. Since each element is appended once and popped at most once, TOTAL `while` pops across entire file <= `N` (`strict O(N)` total time!).",
      "Step 4 (Trigger check cleanly in O(1)): Immediately after pruning expired timestamps, simply check `if len(win) >= 5: flag_burst(...)`!",
      "Step 5 (Pre-compile Regex ONCE Outside Loop!): `PAT = re.compile(...)` BEFORE `for line in f:` (compiling inside a loop wastes huge overhead across 50M lines) — and check cheap `if \"AER\" in line:` substring pre-filter BEFORE running regex!",
      "Step 6 (Verify edge cases!): Handle out-of-order / wrapped midnight timestamps or empty/malformed lines gracefully without crashing mid-parse."
    ],
