    "auditSkill": "Capstone: Detecting & Recovering When Your Own Lab Assumption / Spec Sheet Is Wrong (`Documentation Trap`)",
    "kind": "concept",
    "question": "An engineer spends two days chasing `\"broken I2C/PCIe polarity or flipped VRM scaling\"` because an internal bring-up wiki says Pin 14 is active-HIGH `EN`—yet silicon cleanly powers up ONLY when Pin 14 is pulled LOW! How do you validate ground-truth cleanly when schematic symbols, wikis, and real hardware appear to contradict each other?",
    "visualKind": "soc-blocks",
    "visualParams": {
      "highlight": "When Datasheet/Schematic Disagrees With Silicon -> Probe & Verify Physical Truth!",
      "fault": "Wrong Pinout / Reversed Active-Low Documentation Bug"
    },
    "lesson": "Early A0 bring-up docs and board schematics contain real typos (`swapped P/N silkscreen, flipped active-low bar #, outdated register bit shift`)! **Physical Silicon / Silicon RTL Spec (`RTL Verilog pin ties + Measured I-V curve / scope truth`) outranks human Confluence wikis every time** — verify direction & logic directly by **high-Z resistor pull tests + probing `Pin` voltage unloaded + checking RTL/IP register XML truth**, then immediately file crisp **Errata / Schematic Doc Update** so nobody else burns two days cleanly!",
    "formula": "Wiki/Doc Conflict -> Cross-Check RTL/PDK XML Source + Safe Unloaded Pin Probe -> Update Errata!",
    "sub": "",
    "unit": "concept",
    "unitConv": "Explain with exact SI/EE units, physical mechanism, and bench verification step.",
    "steps": [
      "Step 1 (**Spot Conflict Early — Trust Observations Over Paperwork**): Never ignore repeated consistent behavior (`\"Every board does the opposite of Wiki X!\"`) — consistent reverse behavior screams **Documentation / Schematic / Register-Map Polarity Flip**, NOT random broken hardware!",
      "Step 2 (**Verify Physically & Non-Destructively via Weak Current-Limited Pull!**): Disconnect conflicting driver -> touch Pin 14 gently through a `10 kΩ` resistor (`never hard short to VDD/GND directly!`!) cleanly up to VIO vs down to GND while observing whether pin pulls itself internally (`check weak internal pull-up vs pull-down direction`) AND cleanly reading rail wake!",
      "Step 3 (**Trace Up to Golden Source of Truth — Check RTL/BSDL/Datasheet XML, NOT Secondary Wiki Notes!**): Compare Confluence note against actual **Package Ballout Map (`GDS/Substrate table`), RTL top-level pin polarity (`reset_n`), and Component Vendor Official PDF (`not hand-drawn Altium symbol!`!)**.",
      "Step 4 (**Watch Classic Schematic CAD Trap**: `Swapped QFN Pinout / Mirrored Top-Bottom Header Footprint!!`!): Look carefully at header Pin 1 vs Pin 2 mirror flips (`very common on 2x10 JTAG/board headers soldered upside down in CAD!`).",
      "Step 5 (**Quantify Impact on Other Boards/Teams**): Check if the same wrong symbol or register offset crept into SW headers or ATE test plans cleanly (`grep code/schematics!`).",
      "Step 6 (**Fix Docs Immediately — Leave Campfire Cleaner Than You Found It!**): Submit PR updating bring-up wiki + Errata sheet + schematic symbol immediately cleanly saving dozens of engineering hours later.",
      "Step 7 (**Verify Board Rework Safety Before Shipping Patch!**): If temporary jumper or inverted resistor strap un-breaks Rev A bring-up boards, label each reworked board clearly with bright tag + Rev ECO ID!",
      "Step 8 (**Add Automated Header Check Into Validation Repo!**): Generate Python/C register & GPIO polarity definitions straight from single-source SystemRDL / RTL XML so manual doc drift stops forever!"
    ],
    "rubricKeywords": [
      "trust consistent hardware over wiki",
      "test safely via 10k resistor never direct short",
      "check RTL/Substrate/Vendor PDF truth",
      "mirrored header footprint CAD trap",
      "grep sibling headers",
      "update Errata immediately"
    ],
    "modelAnswer": "Early bring-up docs, schematics, and hand-drawn symbols often carry active-low/high polarity typos or mirrored connector footprints. When all boards behave consistently opposite to a wiki, trust physical measurements: probe pin bias safely via a `10 kΩ` series check (`never hard-short pins!`), audit the true **Vendor datasheet, substrate ballmap, and RTL pin source** (`not secondary wikis!`), check mirrored CAD headers, grep codebase/SW headers for related mistakes, and file immediate Errata/doc updates."
